== 1. Theorem 5.1(d): the bound of (c) is approached ==
Class: f = 0 (weight w) and g_i = 1[x = x_i], i <= N (weight (1-w)/N each); z uniform on N inputs, all L calls at x_z.
  N=10: sum over all rounds of E beta(M^(t)) / L = 2.7836;  ln(1/w) = 4.6052;  two-function example: 1 - w = 0.99
  N=100: sum over all rounds of E beta(M^(t)) / L = 4.1909;  ln(1/w) = 4.6052;  two-function example: 1 - w = 0.99
  N=10000: sum over all rounds of E beta(M^(t)) / L = 4.6003;  ln(1/w) = 4.6052;  two-function example: 1 - w = 0.99
  N=10, n=10: sum_(t<=n) E beta(M^(t)) / L = 0.9829  (limit 2.7836 as n grows; bound ln(1/w) = 4.6052)
  N=10, n=50: sum_(t<=n) E beta(M^(t)) / L = 2.7371  (limit 2.7836 as n grows; bound ln(1/w) = 4.6052)
  N=10, n=200: sum_(t<=n) E beta(M^(t)) / L = 2.7836  (limit 2.7836 as n grows; bound ln(1/w) = 4.6052)
  N=10, n=1000: sum_(t<=n) E beta(M^(t)) / L = 2.7836  (limit 2.7836 as n grows; bound ln(1/w) = 4.6052)

== 2. Example 8.5(d): faults placed blindly ==
L calls at one input; the human answers 1 with probability mu; G = {some answer is 1}; imitator C = always 0.
  L=60 mu=0.05 k=3: human system fails 0.0461; k calls answered blindly: 0.0537; k replacements by a rule that sees the answer: 0.6473; beta/(k+1) = 0.7500; 'delta + beta/(k+1)' with blind tolerance = 0.8037 < 1 = Q_C(not G)
  L=400 mu=0.01 k=39: human system fails 0.0180; k calls answered blindly: 0.0266; k replacements by a rule that sees the answer: 1.0000; beta/(k+1) = 0.1000; 'delta + beta/(k+1)' with blind tolerance = 0.1266 < 1 = Q_C(not G)
  L=4000 mu=0.002 k=399: human system fails 0.0003; k calls answered blindly: 0.0007; k replacements by a rule that sees the answer: 1.0000; beta/(k+1) = 0.0200; 'delta + beta/(k+1)' with blind tolerance = 0.0207 < 1 = Q_C(not G)

== 3. Theorem 13.1(a0): TV(a,p) <= 1 - sum_y a(y)p(y) <= TV(a,p) + 1 - sum_y p(y)^2 ==
  20000 random pairs of distributions: max of TV - dis = 1.11e-16 (<= 0);  max of dis - TV - noise = 1.11e-16 (<= 0);  max of noise/2 - dis = 0.00e+00 (<= 0)

== 4. Proposition 13.4(b): m answers at one input, uniform on Y (M values) against uniform on a random half ==
  M=8 m=3: exact TV = 0.281250 <= m(m-1)/(2M) = 0.375000
  M=64 m=4: exact TV = 0.085854 <= m(m-1)/(2M) = 0.093750
  M=1024 m=8: exact TV = 0.026435 <= m(m-1)/(2M) = 0.027344
  M=1024 m=20: exact TV = 0.142989 <= m(m-1)/(2M) = 0.185547
  M=1048576 m=100: exact TV = 0.004688 <= m(m-1)/(2M) = 0.004721

== 5. Proposition 9.4: Pr[N >= k] <= 2 e^2 L Lambda / k^2 + 2^-k ==
Kernel with the same total variation t at each of L calls (human Bern(1/2), imitator Bern(1/2 - t)); N ~ Bin(L, t).
  12300 cases (L, t, k), of which 8148 have a bound below 1: largest value of exact tail / bound among these = 0.0852 (<= 1)
  the bound 2 e^2 K ln2/(theta^2 n) beats sqrt(K ln2/(2n))/theta for an excess failure below 1/(8 e^2) = 0.0169

== 6. Proposition 5.6 (R7): one call per run at an input uniform on N values ==
  N=50 n=10: fixed input: 0.0183; fixed set of 10 inputs: 0.0893; both <= L/n = 0.1000;  'the set of inputs not asked so far' (depends on the data): 0.9146
  N=200 n=20: fixed input: 0.0048; fixed set of 10 inputs: 0.0321; both <= L/n = 0.0500;  'the set of inputs not asked so far' (depends on the data): 0.9539
  N=1000 n=50: fixed input: 0.0010; fixed set of 10 inputs: 0.0079; both <= L/n = 0.0200;  'the set of inputs not asked so far' (depends on the data): 0.9759

== 7. Constants ==
  Proposition 6.3: abar/sqrt(L) = 0.388355; abar/N <= 0.2388114 (so 0.23882, not 0.23881); bracket >= 0.34796 > 0.3479
  Proposition 6.4: with n = c * 2 L^2 K ln2 the term 2 L B0 (1 + ln(1/(2 B0))) = (1 + ln(L c))/c is below 1 only for c > 4.89  (L = 10; ln L + 3 = 5.30)
  Proposition 6.4: with n = c * 2 L^2 K ln2 the term 2 L B0 (1 + ln(1/(2 B0))) = (1 + ln(L c))/c is below 1 only for c > 7.64  (L = 100; ln L + 3 = 7.61)
  Proposition 6.4: with n = c * 2 L^2 K ln2 the term 2 L B0 (1 + ln(1/(2 B0))) = (1 + ln(L c))/c is below 1 only for c > 10.23  (L = 1000; ln L + 3 = 9.91)
  Corollary 13.2: ln(1/delta)/delta' = 30.0 for delta = 0.05, delta' = 0.1
  Remark 12.3(v): L=1000 eta=0.5 delta=0.01, mu=1, D=0: the condition admits k = 308 with the constant 8 and k = 404 with the constant 2; on the early-stopping scaffold one replaced answer makes the run bad
  Remark 12.3(v): L=100 eta=0.9 delta=0.1, mu=1, D=0: the condition admits k = 47 with the constant 8 and k = 68 with the constant 2; on the early-stopping scaffold one replaced answer makes the run bad
  Budget (K bits, one hour per answer, 2000 hours a year), R1 with L = 10 and failure 0.1: K=1e2: 3.47 years; K=1e4: 347 years; K=1e9: 3.47e+07 years
  Certificate: m = ln(1/0.05)/(0.1*0.1) = 300 rounds certify an excess failure 0.1 under theta = 0.1, whatever L and K

== 8. Proposition 5.7 (audits that are not uniform): an exact example ==
Two calls per run at inputs x1, x2; f = 0; class {f, g1, g2}, g_i wrong at x_i only; weights w(f)=0.2, w(g1)=0.3, w(g2)=0.5.
Audit probabilities (pi1, pi2) with pi1 + pi2 = 1; exact sum over rounds of E[pi1 e1 + pi2 e2] against ln(1/w(f)).
  pi1=0.5: sum = 1.0571 <= ln(1/w(f)) = 1.6094
  pi1=0.9: sum = 1.0229 <= ln(1/w(f)) = 1.6094
  pi1=0.1: sum = 1.0914 <= ln(1/w(f)) = 1.6094

